  to this task.

  [3mAssumption: the static server you use serves [38;5;153m.mjs[39m as a JavaScript MIME type; validate by loading the page once after [0m
  [3mthe change.[0m If it doesn't, the fallback is converting the project to [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m, which pulls those two [38;5;153msrc/[39m
  files into scope.

  [1mThe interface.[0m One engine function plus rule factories:

  [32m// A rule is: (value, allData) => string | null   (null = passes)[39m
  [34mexport[39m [34mfunction[39m [33mrequired[39m(message)
  [34mexport[39m [34mfunction[39m [33mminLength[39m(n, message)
  [34mexport[39m [34mfunction[39m [33mpattern[39m(re, message)
  [34mexport[39m [34mfunction[39m [33mvalidate[39m(data, schema)  [32m// => { valid, errors }[39m

  [38;5;153mvalidate[39m walks each field in the schema, runs its rules in order, and records the [1mfirst[0m failing message for that
  field. [38;5;153mvalid[39m is true when no field produced an error.

  [1mThe built-in rule set:[0m [38;5;153mrequired[39m, [38;5;153mminLength[39m, [38;5;153mpattern[39m — three primitives, about three lines each. Login only needs
  [38;5;153mrequired[39m today; the other two are the ones nearly every form reaches for and are cheap enough that adding them now
  beats a second extraction later. Say the word if you'd rather ship [38;5;153mrequired[39m alone.

  [1mCross-field rules need no built-in.[0m Because rules receive [38;5;153mallData[39m as the second argument, a confirm-password check is
  just an inline arrow function in the schema. No registry, no special case — which is the main reason I picked this
  shape over the descriptor schema.

  Login's schema becomes:

  [34mconst[39m loginSchema = {
    [36musername[39m: [[33mrequired[39m([31m"Username is required"[39m)],
    [36mpassword[39m: [[33mrequired[39m([31m"Password is required"[39m)],
  };

  Does this look right so far?

